nt decline in numbers, and played a significant role in the development of the Renaissance and the modern era.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-07T09:53:16Z", "editorId": "Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct·cloud·GH#27089070728", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "1346–1353 pandemic in eurasia and north africa Took place in Europe. Date: 1346. Ended: 1352.", "newValue": "The Black Death, also known as the Plague, was a pandemic that swept through Europe and other parts of the world in the 14th century, from 1346 to 1353. It is considered one of the deadliest pandemics in human history, causing the deaths of an estimated 75 to 200 million people, roughly 30-60% of Europe's population. The pandemic was caused by the bubonic plague, a highly infectious and deadly disease caused by the bacterium Yersinia pestis. The plague spread rapidly throughout Europe, fueled by trade routes, urbanization, and the lack of medical understanding and treatment. The pandemic had a profound impact on European society, leading to significant social, economic, and cultural changes.

